# Init-neutral Saphira system base.
#
# Intentions ONLY: the package manager owns dependency closure. Never
# enumerate resolved dependencies here; prove the closure with the
# repository index instead (see saphira-profiles(5)).
#
# There is deliberately NO init system in this profile. saphira-base is
# init-neutral, so the same base serves an OpenRC machine and a systemd one
# and the difference is stated once, in init-openrc or init-systemd. A base
# that named an init would force every other profile to restate it.
#
# The profile is named base-system, not saphira-base, because saphira-base is
# an APK. @base-system (this file) and saphira-base (a package) would
# otherwise be two things one keystroke apart, in documentation, in logs and
# in an installer UI. Compose it with @init-openrc or @init-systemd for a
# bare base, or name a product profile (@minimal-openrc, @saphira-openrc)
# for a complete machine.
#
# The kernel is pinned by LINE, not by version: saphira-kernel-7.2.3 is a
# per-kver co-installable identity, so naming it is a bare-package intention
# (recipe-rules.sh compliant) that still selects the known-good 7.2.3 line.
# The bare saphira-kernel identity is disabled (superseded by the per-kver
# split) and must never be named here.
saphira-base
saphira-kernel-7.2.3
# Boot infrastructure is an explicit intention: an installed machine
# carries its own bootloader, both to boot now and to survive a later
# kernel upgrade.
grub
# Filesystem and boot-partition tooling. A machine has to be able to make
# and check the filesystems it lives on.
e2fsprogs
dosfstools
efibootmgr
# Access: a machine with no remote access is not manageable, and a
# headless server with no editor is not Saphira. openssh is ONE package
# here, carrying both the client and sshd; there is no openssh-server.
openssh
vim
# Networking: enough to configure an interface and resolve a name.
iproute2
ca-certificates
tzdata
# Device management: both inits need the udev daemon (the systemd
# package explicitly does not ship systemd-udevd; see its
# strict-complement rule), and /dev/disk/* never exists without it
# (proven Egg 2026-10-19). Init-neutral like everything else here:
# openrc runs /etc/init.d/udev, systemd runs systemd-udevd.service.
udev
